HLS coordinator startup and failure handling
==========================================

Workers on the same Linux host coordinate through a private Unix socket and an
exclusive election lock. A follower may arrive while the elected owner is still
starting. It now waits for connection establishment within the existing
20-second request budget, instead of abandoning startup after one 10 ms retry.
Only missing/refused connections are retried. Invalid socket ownership or
permissions remain refusals. Once any command bytes have been submitted, the
command is never replayed after a lost or invalid response.

The owner binds a unique temporary socket inside the private media directory,
sets mode 0600, starts listening, and atomically publishes the normal control
socket name. It does not change the process-wide umask. Interrupted startup
closes the owned server/socket, removes only the temporary socket inode it
created, and releases its election descriptors. Existing unsafe entries and
unrelated files are not removed by this cleanup.

The original full-suite failure is retained: two concurrent API workers returned
200 and 503, producing 18 setup errors in one shared fixture. New regressions
deliberately delay lock acquisition completion, permission setup, and listening
in separate OS processes. They require both API starts to succeed, share one
session and media child, and report two viewers through the heartbeat endpoint.
Additional checks exercise deadline expiry, cancellation, and failed startup
cleanup. Synthetic camera identity, authority and media generation are explicit
fixtures; these checks do not prove real authentication or camera delivery.

This is coordination for workers sharing one local media directory and trusted
application UID. It is not cross-host routing, high availability, or seamless
recovery of an active stream after owner or host failure. A process crash can
leave private temporary socket entries; this change does not claim a general
filesystem retention or quota policy.

